
  [1mThe fork:[0m where the [38;5;153muserId[39m actually comes from. [38;5;153mlogin()[39m today is a stub — [38;5;153mAPI_ENDPOINT[39m is declared on line 2 and never
  used, and the function synchronously returns [38;5;153m{ success: true, user: username }[39m. Whether we change that determines
  whether the whole subsystem is synchronous or async, and async reaches everything: the submit handler, error handling,
  and every consumer that reads the session during an in-flight login.

  - [1mKeep the stub; derive [38;5;153muserId[39m from the username[0m — [38;5;153mlogin()[39m stays synchronous, the session stores the username as the
    identifier. Smallest design, no network, no error states. The seam is built so a real call drops in later, but
    "later" means revisiting every caller to make it async.
  - [1mWire the real [38;5;153mPOST[39m to [38;5;153mAPI_ENDPOINT[39m; server returns the [38;5;153muserId[0m — the honest version: the server authenticates and
    tells you who that was. Makes [38;5;153mlogin()[39m async, which forces the submit handler and consumers to deal with pending and
    failed states. Bigger, and it can't be tested against a server that may not exist.
  - [1mStub now, but make the interface async from day one[0m — [38;5;153mlogin()[39m returns a Promise that currently resolves immediately
    from the stub. Costs a little ceremony now; means swapping in the real call later touches one function instead of
    every caller.

  [1mMy recommendation:[0m the third. The async/sync split is the single most expensive thing to change after callers exist —
  it's a rewrite of every call site, and you've told me call sites are coming ("other forms will need it later"). Paying
  for a Promise-shaped interface now while the body is still a stub costs almost nothing and buys the one migration
  that would otherwise hurt. The first option is cheaper today and I'd take it only if you're confident this stays a
  fixture.
